# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CineDeck environment
# Copy to .env and fill in. NEVER commit .env.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL
#
# Development (SQLite, default):
#   DATABASE_URL=file:/absolute/path/to/db/custom.db
#   or relative to the prisma folder:
#   DATABASE_URL="file:../db/custom.db"
#
# Production (PostgreSQL):
#   1. In prisma/schema.prisma change the datasource provider:
#        provider = "postgresql"
#   2. Point DATABASE_URL at your Postgres instance:
#        DATABASE_URL=postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/cinedeck?schema=public
#   3. Run `bunx prisma migrate deploy` (never db push) against the database.
#
# The model layer is portable between both providers — no engine-specific
# types are used anywhere in the schema.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
DATABASE_URL=file:/home/z/my-project/db/custom.db

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUTH_SECRET (REQUIRED in production)
#
# 32+ byte random hex string used to sign stateless auth tokens (password
# reset, email verification, TOTP pending-login). Sessions themselves are
# random tokens hashed in the database.
#
# Generate one:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Behavior when missing/too short:
#   - default: the server self-heals — a stable random secret is generated
#     and written into .env on first run (a warning is logged). The app must
#     never crash at build/start because of a missing optional env var.
#   - STABILITY CONTRACT (Task 44): a valid secret is NEVER regenerated.
#     The first boot also stores it in the SystemState DB table; if a
#     platform reset later wipes .env, the boot hook (and `bun run deploy`)
#     restore the ORIGINAL secret from the DB, so outstanding reset /
#     verification links keep working and nothing needs to be re-issued.
#     `bun run doctor` compares a SHA-256 fingerprint and warns if the
#     secret ever changed. Sessions are DB-backed and unaffected either way.
#   - PUBLISH_STRICT=1 (AND NODE_ENV=production): the server refuses to boot.
#     Use this for hardened deployments where an operator manages secrets.
# Rotating it invalidates outstanding reset/verification links (sessions are
# unaffected).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
AUTH_SECRET=replace-me-with-openssl-rand-hex-32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PUBLISH_STRICT (optional, default unset)
#
# Set PUBLISH_STRICT=1 only in hardened production deployments. Combined with
# NODE_ENV=production it enforces hard-fail behavior for missing secrets
# (AUTH_SECRET). Leave unset for normal platform publish/preview flows.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PUBLISH_STRICT=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TMDB_API_KEY (optional)
#
# Server-wide TMDB v3 key. When unset, CineDeck runs fully keyless via
# Cinemeta and each user can paste their own free TMDB key in Settings
# (stored in the browser, sent per-request via the x-tmdb-key header).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TMDB_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SMTP / email provider (optional)
#
# Password reset + email verification links are delivered through the email
# adapter in src/lib/server/mailer.ts. Without SMTP credentials the adapter
# logs links to the server console (dev-friendly, never silently drops).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SMTP_URL=smtp://user:pass@smtp.example.com:587
# MAIL_FROM="CineDeck <no-reply@cinedeck.example>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# E2E_RATELIMIT_BYPASS (optional, dev-only)
#
# Shared secret enabling the Playwright e2e suite to bypass in-memory rate
# limits on repeated runs. Only honored when ALL of these hold: the server
# runs with NODE_ENV != production, the request originates from loopback,
# and the header `x-cinedeck-e2e` matches this value.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# E2E_RATELIMIT_BYPASS=generate-with-openssl-rand-hex-16

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADMIN SEED (ADMIN_EMAIL + ADMIN_PASSWORD)
#
# When both are set, the app automatically creates this admin account on
# first boot (idempotent — running again NEVER overwrites the account or
# resets its password). Also runnable manually: `bun run seed`.
#
# FALLBACK: when the env vars are missing, the seed reads the same two
# values from config/bootstrap-admin.json (project files survive sandbox
# restarts that regenerate .env). Edit that file or these env vars —
# whichever you prefer; env wins when both are present.
#
# Signups are ALWAYS role=user. The only admin is the seeded account —
# no first-user escalation exists.
# Set ADMIN_PASSWORD to something strong; the seeded account gets
# mustChangePassword=true so the UI nags until the password is changed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ADMIN_EMAIL=
ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADMIN_IP_ALLOWLIST (optional — admin logins only)
#
# Comma-separated list of client IPs allowed to sign in to role="admin"
# accounts. Admin logins from any other IP are rejected with
# "Login not allowed from this network." Leave UNSET (or empty) to skip the
# check entirely — dev and hosted previews keep working.
# Admin accounts are also limited to 3 active device sessions; a 4th login is
# rejected with an actionable message (see src/lib/server/admin-security.ts).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADMIN_IP_ALLOWLIST=62.78.226.59

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS (optional — CSRF origin allowlist additions)
#
# Extra hostnames (comma-separated, no scheme/port) that mutating API
# requests may come from, on top of the Host/X-Forwarded-Host headers and
# the built-in z.ai preview platform domains. Modern browsers are covered
# by Sec-Fetch-Site regardless; this is the escape hatch for exotic
# reverse-proxy setups and custom domains.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=deck.example.com,www.example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EMAIL_VERIFICATION=on
#   Makes registration respond with a generic "verification sent" payload
#   for ALL addresses (anti account-enumeration). Off = dev default.
# SMTP_URL=smtp://user:pass@host:port
#   Real mail delivery. Unset = console mail adapter (links in server logs).
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID / GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET
#   Optional Google OAuth. Unset = the option is hidden everywhere.
# APP_BASE_URL=https://yourapp.z.ai
#   Absolute base URL used in auth emails. Auto-detected from requests when
#   unset.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY HARDENING (2026-11-03) — new / changed variables
#
# APP_BASE_URL (STRONGLY RECOMMENDED in production)
#   Absolute public origin used in emailed reset/verification links. When
#   unset, links are derived from request Host headers — safe behind the
#   platform gateway, but poisoned-linkable on direct exposure. Set it to
#   the deployment's public origin, e.g.:
# APP_BASE_URL=https://movieplayer222.space-z.ai
#
# TRUST_PROXY (default: trusted)
#   X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP are trusted because the platform Caddy
#   gateway REPLACES the forwarded chain (client values cannot survive it).
#   Set TRUST_PROXY=0 only when the app is directly exposed and rate limits
#   should key on the socket peer instead.
# TRUST_PROXY=0
#
# ADMIN_REQUIRE_TOTP (default: unset)
#   Set to 1 to REFUSE admin logins until the account has 2FA enrolled.
#   Off by default so an existing admin without TOTP is never locked out —
#   enable it after enrolling 2FA on every admin account.
# ADMIN_REQUIRE_TOTP=1
#
# DEBUG_PRISMA (default: unset)
#   Set to 1 to restore Prisma query logging (bound parameters — including
#   hashes — print to the log; dev debugging only).
# DEBUG_PRISMA=1
#
# SECURITY MODEL NOTES
#   - AUTH_SECRET: runtime env / project .env / protected volume file
#     (/tmp/my-project/config/auth-secret, 0600). NEVER in the database —
#     only a SHA-256 fingerprint is stored for drift detection.
#   - TOTP secrets: encrypted at rest with AES-256-GCM under an HKDF key
#     derived from AUTH_SECRET (key and ciphertext stored separately).
#   - Rate limits: persistent SQLite token buckets (RateLimitState table) —
#     survive restarts; auth endpoints also carry per-ACCOUNT buckets.
#   - CSRF: double-submit token cookie (cinedeck_csrf, set by middleware) +
#     x-csrf-token header on every mutating fetch + strict origin checks.
#   - CSP: per-request nonce + strict-dynamic in production (no
#     unsafe-inline / unsafe-eval for scripts); unsafe-eval remains ONLY in
#     development for Next.js tooling.
#   - Session cookies: HttpOnly + SameSite=Lax + Secure (production), with
#     absolute expiry (60d members / 7d admins) and immediate revocation on
#     suspension or deletion.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
